# Link — a label and a destination the CALLER supplies. This is the facet the
# whole `navigation/` category was blocked on: until the compiler learned the
# route-expression form, `link "{label}" -> "{href}"` was rejected outright
# (*link path must start with `/`*), and a navigation atom that cannot be told
# where to point is not an atom, it is a hard-coded menu.
#
# A destination is now one of exactly two things:
#
#   * a PATH TEMPLATE — starts with a literal `/`, e.g. "/post/{p.id}". The
#     compiler knows its shape, checks it against the app's routes, and
#     percent-escapes what fills the holes. A call site writing its own link
#     should keep using this form: a typo is a build failure.
#   * a ROUTE EXPRESSION — one whole interpolation and nothing else, "{href}".
#     Nothing static can say where it points, so the check moves to the
#     renderers: they resolve the value against the routes this app serves and
#     emit an `<a>` only if it names one. Otherwise the label renders as an
#     inert `<span class="fa-link">`.
#
# There is deliberately nothing in between — "{base}/edit" is refused — because a
# half-written destination would have to be half-escaped, and a rule that escapes
# some of a path and not the rest is the rule that lets a value out of its segment.
#
# HOW A CALL SITE BUILDS A DESTINATION, since this is the trap the whole category
# walks into. A component ARGUMENT is an expression, and a quoted `{p.id}` in
# expression position is a string literal containing a brace, a p, a dot, an id and
# a brace. Interpolation is a property of a node's text, not of a string. So a
# destination assembled at the call site is assembled with `+`, which concatenates
# when either side is text and converts the other:
#
#     use Link("Open", "/post/" + p.id)          # correct
#     link "Open" -> "/post/{p.id}"              # correct, and route-checked
#
# THE THIRD FORM — an interpolation inside the argument — IS A BUILD ERROR NOW.
# It used to compile clean and render the characters, and it took a real
# storefront's product grid down to `href="/p/{p.id}"` and visible text reading
# `{p.name}` before anyone noticed. The compiler refuses it when the braces name
# something the surrounding scope actually defines, and names the argument and the
# fix; braces around a name the scope does not define are still literal text
# everywhere, so prose and JSON samples are untouched.
#
# The second line is the one to prefer whenever the call site is WRITING the path:
# it is the template form, so a typo is a build failure rather than a link that
# turns into inert text at runtime. `Link` earns its keep where the destination
# ARRIVES as data — a menu row's `href` field, a route parameter, a cell — which
# is precisely the case that was unwritable before.
#
# THE DEGRADED FORM IS THE FEATURE, not a fallback to apologise for. A component
# handed a dead destination renders the words and refuses to promise navigation,
# so a link in this library can never dangle: it either goes somewhere this app
# actually serves, or it visibly is not a link. The `span.x-link` rule below is
# what makes that legible rather than a mystery — the text goes muted and loses
# its pointer, so a broken destination looks broken in the browser instead of
# looking fine until it is clicked.
app FacetNav:
    css:
        [data-fa-mount] a.x-link { color: var(--fa-accent); text-decoration: none; }
        [data-fa-mount] a.x-link:hover { text-decoration: underline; }
        # The inert half of a route expression. Same size and weight as the live
        # one — this is not an error state — but muted and unclickable, so the
        # difference reads as "nowhere to go", not as "styling forgot this one".
        [data-fa-mount] span.x-link { color: var(--fa-muted); cursor: default; }
    component Link(label: text, href: text):
        link "{label}" -> "{href}" class "x-link"
